  "overview": {
    "text": "Deuterium (hydrogen-2, written ²H or D) is a stable, naturally occurring but minor isotope of hydrogen. Its nucleus has one proton and one neutron; the most common hydrogen isotope, protium, has only a proton, so deuterium is roughly twice as massive. Its atomic mass is about 2.014, and its proportion varies among natural hydrogen sources. Deuterium is important in research: fusion scientists investigate combining it with radioactive tritium, a reaction that produces helium and an energetic neutron, as a possible route to future power. The isotope’s experimental identification in 1931 provided striking evidence that hydrogen, too, has isotopes.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://ciaaw.org/hydrogen.htm",
      "https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainsdeuterium-tritium-fusion-fuel",
      "https://www.nist.gov/history/nbsnist-culture-excellence/harold-c-urey-ferdinand-g-brickwedde-and-discovery-deuterium"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "Deuterium is the minor stable isotope ²H; its symbol is D, and its listed atomic mass is about 2.014 Da.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://ciaaw.org/hydrogen.htm"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A deuterium nucleus contains one proton and one neutron. The most common hydrogen isotope, protium, has one proton and no neutrons.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainsdeuterium-tritium-fusion-fuel"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Deuterium’s natural abundance varies among hydrogen sources; CIAAW lists a range rather than one universal percentage.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://ciaaw.org/hydrogen.htm"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Experiments reported in 1932 established hydrogen-2’s existence; Harold Urey, George Murphy, and Ferdinand Brickwedde identified its spectral lines in enriched samples.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.nist.gov/history/nbsnist-culture-excellence/harold-c-urey-ferdinand-g-brickwedde-and-discovery-deuterium"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Deuterium-tritium fusion is being investigated as a possible future energy source; the reaction produces helium and an energetic neutron.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainsdeuterium-tritium-fusion-fuel"
      ]
    }
  ],
